{
  "entity0": {
    "rationale": "This is the first iteration, so there is no race telemetry or Integrator feedback yet. I am setting a baseline that is symmetric, centred on the line, and placed well within the allowed limits. The evidence available is the contract itself: the tape is 25 mm wide, and the course needs an average of about 1.5 m/s to finish inside 3 s. Five sensors at 8 mm spacing span 32 mm, so the tape is always covered by at least two sensors and the signal stays continuous across a centred line. Placing the sensors 5 cm ahead of the axle gives the controller look-ahead, so it can react to bends before the wheels reach them. Weights of -2, -1, 0, 1, 2 map the sensor's sideways position directly onto the signal, with positive x (left) giving a positive signal, which steers left, as the contract requires. A 12 mm sensor footprint is a compromise between resolution and smooth transitions. A wheel track of 0.12 m and wheel radius of 0.03 m give a moderate turn authority. Mass 0.7 kg keeps yaw inertia low, so the robot can turn quickly. The body width of 0.12 m is well clear of the off-track limit, which is set by the 0.06 m floor. I expect this baseline to finish most development tracks, with the main failure risk on the sharpest slalom bends, where the look-ahead may be too short. I am not yet sure whether the sensor band is wide enough to avoid line-loss events on tight bends.",
    "geometry": {
      "sensor_positions": [
        {"x": -0.016, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": -0.008, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": 0.008, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": 0.016, "y": 0.05}
      ],
      "sensor_size": 0.012,
      "wheel_track": 0.12,
      "wheel_radius": 0.03,
      "body_width": 0.12,
      "body_length": 0.18,
      "mass": 0.7
    },
    "intent": "Baseline symmetric five-sensor array, 5 cm ahead of the axle, 8 mm spacing across a 32 mm band, with linear weights -2 to +2 so the signal scales with lateral offset. Compact, low-inertia body with moderate track and wheel size. Purpose: give the Integrator a clean, centred platform to tune on, and establish whether the look-ahead distance and sensor band width are adequate on the development tracks. Expected result: completion on oval and s-bend, with the main risk on slalom bends.",
    "feedback_request": {
      "questions": [
        "Does the five-sensor band of 32 mm give a stable, monotonic signal across the tape, or are there dead zones or saturation at the edges?",
        "Is 5 cm of look-ahead enough for the controller to act before the line leaves the sensors on the sharpest bends?",
        "Does the 0.7 kg mass with a 12 cm track give enough turn authority at base_speed near 2, or does the robot understeer?",
        "Which sensor weights or spacing changes would reduce jitter or line-loss events, as far as you can tell from the geometry alone?"
      ]
    },
    "learning": "No prior race. Expect the first telemetry to show whether the look-ahead and band width are the limiting factors, which would decide whether the next iteration widens the array or moves the sensors forward.",
    "_geometry_note": "Baseline is intentionally conservative."
  },
  "_presentation": {
    "entity0": {
      "label": "Baseline 5-sensor array",
      "notes": "A symmetric five-sensor baseline, 5 cm ahead of the axle, with 8 mm spacing and linear weights, to give the Integrator a centred platform. No race evidence yet, so it rests on the contract's line width and speed targets."
    }
  }
}
